CM_ "FrameID: 0x418";
BO_ 1048 Steering_Wheel_Heating: 8 Vector__XXX
 SG_ Steering_Wheel_Heating_State : 24|1@1+ (1,0) [0|1] "" Vector__XXX
CM_ BO_ 1048 "a.k.a. ICU ICU_08_200ms in gen5w_hu_rx.dbc";
CM_ SG_ 1048 Steering_Wheel_Heating_State "byte 3 bit 0. Previously modelled at 16|8 (byte 2), which is 0x00 in 100% of every capture on every car (Ioniq 5 5731, Ioniq 6 1916, GV60 1063 frames), so it could only ever read Off. Byte 3 bit 0 is the only bit that tracks the steering-wheel warmer: 0x01 in M-CAN_steering_wheel_warmer_only.csv (10/20 frames), M-CAN_panda_nothing_on_climate_start_both_warmers.csv (60/202) and M-CAN_panda_nothing_on_remote_lock.csv (16/100), and 0x00 in every other capture - including the dedicated left/right seat warmer, climate-on, climate-on-defrost and rear-defrost files, so it is not a generic warmer flag. Byte 5 bit 6 (0x0C -> 0x4C, i.e. bit 45) is the generic one: it turns on with ANY warmer and is a separate candidate signal. The steering-wheel captures are only a few seconds long, so whether byte 3 bit 0 is a latched state or the press event itself is not settled.";
CM_ SG_ 1048 Steering_Wheel_Heating_State "DIFFERENCE ON CCU CARS. This bit-0 encoding is only demonstrated on the Ioniq 5 (NE, ICU CAN hub), and likely works for the EV6 as well. The CCU-hub cars do NOT show it: every Ioniq 6 (1916 frames) and GV60 (1063 frames) sample has byte 3 = 0x40, i.e. bit 6 SET and bit 0 CLEAR, so bit 0 would decode a permanent Off there and the state - if it is reported at all - is carried elsewhere in the byte. Do not apply this signal to a CCU-hub capture (load ioniq6-2024.dbc / gv60-2024.dbc for those).";

CM_ "FrameID: 0x438";
